{"id":"book-iii-translator-and-historical-edition-notes","label":"Book III. — Translator and historical edition notes","headingNoteRefs":"","context":"Book III.","kind":"commentary","blocks":[{"text":"[^n1034]: Ps. lxxiii. 1.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1034"},{"text":"[^n1035]: [See Exhortation to the Heathen, cap. xi. p. 203, supra.]","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1035"},{"text":"[^n1036]: The pædagogus. [This word seems to be used by Clement, with frequent alusion, at least, to its original idea, of one who leads the child to his instructor; which is the true idea, I suppose, in Gal. iii. 24.]","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1036"},{"text":"[^n1037]: Num. vi. 9.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1037"},{"text":"[^n1038]: Num. vi. 12.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1038"},{"text":"[^n1039]: Ps. lxxxvi. 2, 3.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1039"},{"text":"[^n1040]: Mark ii. 11.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1040"},{"text":"[^n1041]: John xi. 43.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1041"},{"text":"[^n1042]: Matt. ix. 2.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1042"},{"text":"[^n1043]: Bishop Kaye (Some Account of the Writings and Opinions of Clement of Alexandria, p. 48) translates, “receiving from man that which made man (that on account of which man was made).” But it seems more likely that Clement refers to the ideal man in the divine mind, whom he indentifies elsewhere with the Logos, the ἄνθρωπος ἀπαθής, of whom man was the image. The reader will notice that Clement speaks of man as existing in the divine mind before his creation, and creation is represented by God’s seeing what He had previously within Him merely as a hidden power.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1043"},{"text":"[^n1044]: John xvi. 27.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1044"},{"text":"[^n1045]: John xvii. 23.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1045"},{"text":"[^n1046]: Matt. xv. 14.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1046"},{"text":"[^n1047]: John i. 14.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1047"},{"text":"[^n1048]: Luke xx. 34.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1048"},{"text":"[^n1049]: John xxi. 4, 5.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1049"},{"text":"[^n1050]: Matt. xix. 14.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1050"},{"text":"[^n1051]: Matt. xviii. 3.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1051"},{"text":"[^n1052]: [The dignity ascribed to Christian childhood in this chapter is something noteworthy. The Gospel glorifying children, sanctifies marriage, and creates the home.]","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1052"},{"text":"[^n1053]: Matt. xxi. 9.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1053"},{"text":"[^n1054]: Matt. xxi. 16; Ps. viii. 2.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1054"},{"text":"[^n1055]: John xiii. 33.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1055"},{"text":"[^n1056]: Matt. xi. 16, 17. [In the Peshitoi-Syraic version, where are probably found the very words our Saviour thus quotes from children in Nazareth, this saying is seen to be metrical and alliterative.]","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1056"},{"text":"[^n1057]: Ps. cxiii. 1.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1057"},{"text":"[^n1058]: Isa. viii. 18.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1058"},{"text":"[^n1059]: Matt. xxv. 33.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1059"},{"text":"[^n1060]: Matt. x. 16.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1060"},{"text":"[^n1061]: Lev. xv. 29, xii. 8; Luke ii. 24.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1061"},{"text":"[^n1062]: Matt. xxiii. 37.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1062"},{"text":"[^n1063]: Isa. lxv. 15, 16.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1063"},{"text":"[^n1064]: Jer. v. 8.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1064"},{"text":"[^n1065]: Zech. ix. 9; Gen. xlix. 11.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1065"},{"text":"[^n1066]: Isa. xl. 11.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1066"},{"text":"[^n1067]: Matt. xviii. 4.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1067"},{"text":"[^n1068]: Theodoret explains this to mean that, as the animal referred to has only one horn, so those brought up in the practice of piety worship only one God. [It might mean lovers of those promises which are introduced by these words in the marvellous twenty-second Psalm.]","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1068"},{"text":"[^n1069]: Matt. vi. 34.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1069"},{"text":"[^n1070]: Ps. v. 6.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1070"},{"text":"[^n1071]: 2 Cor. xi. 2.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1071"},{"text":"[^n1072]: Eph. iv. 13–15.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1072"},{"text":"[^n1073]: 1 Thess. ii. 6, 7.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1073"},{"text":"[^n1074]: Isa. lxvi. 2.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1074"},{"text":"[^n1075]: Rom. xvi. 19.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1075"},{"text":"[^n1076]: Matt. xi. 27; Luke x. 22.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1076"},{"text":"[^n1077]: Isa. lxvi. 12, 13.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1077"},{"text":"[^n1078]: Gen. xxvi. 8.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1078"},{"text":"[^n1079]: Isa. ix. 6.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1079"},{"text":"[^n1080]: Luke vii. 28.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1080"},{"text":"[^n1081]: John i. 29, 36.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1081"},{"text":"[^n1082]: In allusion apparently to John viii. 35, 36.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1082"},{"text":"[^n1083]: Ps. lxxxii. 6.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1083"},{"text":"[^n1084]: χάρισμα","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1084"},{"text":"[^n1085]: John i. 4.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1085"},{"text":"[^n1086]: John v. 24.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1086"},{"text":"[^n1087]: viz., the result of His will.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1087"},{"text":"[^n1088]: 1 Thess. iv. 9.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1088"},{"text":"[^n1089]: Eph. v. 8.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1089"},{"text":"[^n1090]: φως, light; φώς, a man.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1090"},{"text":"[^n1091]: John vi. 40.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1091"},{"text":"[^n1092]: John iii. 36.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1092"},{"text":"[^n1093]: Matt. ix. 29.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1093"},{"text":"[^n1094]: Migne’s text has ἀποκάλυψις. The emendation ἀπόληψις is preferable.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1094"},{"text":"[^n1095]: [Iliad, v. 401.]","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1095"},{"text":"[^n1096]: Gal. iii. 23–25. [Here the schoolmaster should be the child-guide; for the law leads us to the Master, says Clement, and we are no longer under the disciplinary guide, but “under the Word, the master of our free choice.” The schoolmaster then is the Word, and the law merely led us to his school.]","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1096"},{"text":"[^n1097]: Gal. iii. 26–28.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1097"},{"text":"[^n1098]: 1 Cor. xii. 13.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1098"},{"text":"[^n1099]: Luke x. 21.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1099"},{"text":"[^n1100]: Luke x. 21.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1100"},{"text":"[^n1101]: [Clement here considers all believers as babes, in the sense he explains; but the tenderness towards children of the allusions running through this chapter are not the less striking.]","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1101"},{"text":"[^n1102]: 1 Cor. xiv. 20.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1102"},{"text":"[^n1103]: 1 Cor. xiii. 11. [A text much misused by the heretical gnostics whom Clement confutes.]","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1103"},{"text":"[^n1104]: viz., simple or innocent as a child, and foolish as a child.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1104"},{"text":"[^n1105]: 1 Cor. xiii. 11.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1105"},{"text":"[^n1106]: Gal. iv. 1–5.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1106"},{"text":"[^n1107]: Gal. iv. 7.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1107"},{"text":"[^n1108]: 1 Cor. iii. 2.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1108"},{"text":"[^n1109]: Ex. iii. 8.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1109"},{"text":"[^n1110]: Rev. i. 8.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1110"},{"text":"[^n1111]: [Iliad, xiii. 6. S.]","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1111"},{"text":"[^n1112]: 1 Cor. iii. 1.","kind":"commentary","note