x. 7.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1291"},{"text":"[^n1292]: Gal. iii. 24.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1292"},{"text":"[^n1293]: Matt. xvii. 5.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1293"},{"text":"[^n1294]: John i. 3.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1294"},{"text":"[^n1295]: John x. 11.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1295"},{"text":"[^n1296]: Gen. i. 26.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1296"},{"text":"[^n1297]: Matt. vi. 34.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1297"},{"text":"[^n1298]: [The secondary, civilizing, and socializing power of the Gospel, must have already produced all this change from heathen manners, under Clement’s own observation.]","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1298"},{"text":"[^n1299]: Ps. xlix. 12, 20.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1299"},{"text":"[^n1300]: Ecclus. xxxiii. 6.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1300"},{"text":"[^n1301]: [Note this definition in Christian ethics.]","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1301"},{"text":"[^n1302]: Prov. xxiii. 3.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1302"},{"text":"[^n1303]: 1 Cor. vi. 13.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1303"},{"text":"[^n1304]: 1 Cor. vi. 13.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1304"},{"text":"[^n1305]: ὄθεν, an emendation for ὄν.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1305"},{"text":"[^n1306]: Love, or love-feast, a name applied by the ancients to public entertainments. [But surely he is here rebuking, with St. Jude (v. 12), abuses of the Christian agapæ by heretics and others.]","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1306"},{"text":"[^n1307]: Luke xiv. 8, 10.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1307"},{"text":"[^n1308]: Luke xiv. 12, 13.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1308"},{"text":"[^n1309]: Luke xiv. 16.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1309"},{"text":"[^n1310]: 1 Cor. xiii. 7, 8.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1310"},{"text":"[^n1311]: Luke xiv. 15.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1311"},{"text":"[^n1312]: 1 Cor. xiii. 3.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1312"},{"text":"[^n1313]: Rom. xiv. 16, 17.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1313"},{"text":"[^n1314]: Wisd. vi. 17, 18.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1314"},{"text":"[^n1315]: Wisd. xvi. 26.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1315"},{"text":"[^n1316]: Deut. viii. 3; Matt. iv. 4.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1316"},{"text":"[^n1317]: Isa. xxii. 13, 14.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1317"},{"text":"[^n1318]: Ecclus. xviii. 32.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1318"},{"text":"[^n1319]: Odyss., xi. 37.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1319"},{"text":"[^n1320]: 1 Cor. x. 20.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1320"},{"text":"[^n1321]: 1 Cor. viii. 7, 8.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1321"},{"text":"[^n1322]: Matt. xv. 11.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1322"},{"text":"[^n1323]: 1 Cor. viii. 8.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1323"},{"text":"[^n1324]: Luke xv. 11.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1324"},{"text":"[^n1325]: 1 Cor. x. 27.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1325"},{"text":"[^n1326]: 1 Cor. x. 25.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1326"},{"text":"[^n1327]: Rom. xiv. 3.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1327"},{"text":"[^n1328]: Rom. xiv. 6.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1328"},{"text":"[^n1329]: 1 Cor. viii. 13.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1329"},{"text":"[^n1330]: 1 Cor. ix. 14.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1330"},{"text":"[^n1331]: 1 Cor. viii. 6, 11, 12.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1331"},{"text":"[^n1332]: 1 Cor. v. 11.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1332"},{"text":"[^n1333]: Rom. xiv. 21.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1333"},{"text":"[^n1334]: Rom. xiv. 20.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1334"},{"text":"[^n1335]: [Clement seems to think this abuse was connected with the agapæ not—one might trust—with the Lord’s supper.]","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1335"},{"text":"[^n1336]: 1 Cor. xi. 21, 22.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1336"},{"text":"[^n1337]: 1 Cor. xi. 33, 34.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1337"},{"text":"[^n1338]: Literally, “slave-manners,” the conduct to be expected from slaves.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1338"},{"text":"[^n1339]: 1 Cor. x. 31.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1339"},{"text":"[^n1340]: Matt. xxii. 21.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1340"},{"text":"[^n1341]: 1 Cor. x. 23.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1341"},{"text":"[^n1342]: 1 Cor. x. 23.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1342"},{"text":"[^n1343]: Prov. xiii. 5.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1343"},{"text":"[^n1344]: A bulbous root, much prized in Greece, which grew wild.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1344"},{"text":"[^n1345]: Luke xxiv. 41–44.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1345"},{"text":"[^n1346]: A play here on the words εὐδαίμων and δαίμων.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1346"},{"text":"[^n1347]: ἀκρόδρυα, hard-shelled fruits.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1347"},{"text":"[^n1348]: Acts x. 10–15.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1348"},{"text":"[^n1349]: Matt. xv. 11.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1349"},{"text":"[^n1350]: Gen. ix. 2, 3.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1350"},{"text":"[^n1351]: Prov. xv. 17.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1351"},{"text":"[^n1352]: In allusion to the agapæ, or love-feasts.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1352"},{"text":"[^n1353]: 2 Kings vi. 17–19, Septuagint: 2 Sam. vi. 17–19. A.V.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1353"},{"text":"[^n1354]: ὄνος, perhaps the hake or cod.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1354"},{"text":"[^n1355]: Phil. iii. 19.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1355"},{"text":"[^n1356]: 1 Tim. v. 23.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1356"},{"text":"[^n1357]: [This remarkable chapter seems to begin with the author’s recollections of Pindar (ἄριστον μὲν ϋδωρ), but to lay down very justly the Scriptural ideas of temperance and abstinence.]","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1357"},{"text":"[^n1358]: Ex. xvii.; Num. xx.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1358"},{"text":"[^n1359]: [Clement reckons only two classes as living faithfully with respect to drink, the abstinent and the totally abstinent.]","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1359"},{"text":"[^n1360]: [This seems Clement’s exposition of St. John (vi. 63), and a clear statement as to the Eucharist, which he pronounces spiritual food.]","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1360"},{"text":"[^n1361]: [A plain reference to the use of the mixed cup in the Lord’s supper.]","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1361"},{"text":"[^n1362]: [If the temperate do well, he thinks, the abstinent do better; but nobody is temperate who does not often and habitually abstain.]","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1362"},{"text":"[^n1363]: [A very important principle; for, if wine be “the milk of age,” the use of it in youth deprives age of any benefit from its sober use].","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1363"},{"text":"[^n1364]: The exact derivation of acrothorakes is matter of doubt. But we have the authority of Aristotle and Erotian for believing that is was applied to those who were slightly drunk. Some regard the clause here as an interpolation.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1364"},{"text":"[^n1365]: Ecclus. xxxi. 27.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1365"},{"text":"[^n1366]: Pentheus in Euripides, Bacch., 918.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1366"},{"text":"[^n1367]: Attributed to Sophocles.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1367"},{"text":"[^n1368]: Ecclus. xxxi. 29.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1368"},{"text":"[^n1369]: [A beautiful maxim, and proving the habit of early Christians to use completory prayers. This the drunkard is in no state to do.]","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1369"},{"text":"[^n1370]: Ecclus. xxxi. 26.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1370"},{"text":"[^n1371]: Prov. xxiii. 20.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1371"},{"text":"[^n1372]: Prov. xxiii. 21.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1372"},{"text":"[^n1373]: Prov. xxiii. 29, 30.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1373"},{"text":"[^n1374]: [A passage not to be overlooked. Greek, μυστικὸν σύμβολον.]","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1374"},{"text":"[^n1375]: Prov. xx. 1.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1375"},{"text":"[^n1376]: ἀνθοσμίας. Some suppose the word to be derived from the name of a town: “The Anthosmian.”","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1376"},{"text":"[^n1377]: Amos vi. 4, 6.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1377"},{"text":"[^n1378]: [Here Clement satirizes heathen manners, and quote Athene, to shame Christians who imitate them.]","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1378"},{"text":"[^n1379]: Ecclus. xxxi. 25.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1379"},{"text":"[^n1380]: [The blood of the vine is Christ’s blood. According to Clement, then, it remains in the Eucharist unchanged.]","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1380"},{"text":"[^n1381]: Mark xvi. 25; Matt. xxvi. 29. [This also is a noteworthy use of the text.]","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1381"},{"text":"[^n1382]: Matt. xi. 19.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1382"},{"text":"[^n1383]: Ecclus. xxvi. 8.","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1383"},{"text":"[^n1384]: 1 Cor. xi. 20. [Clement has already hinted his opinion, that this referred to a shameful custom of the Corinthians to let an agape precede the Eucharist; an abuse growing out of our Lord’s eating of the Passover before he instituted the Eucharist.]","kind":"commentary","noteId":"n1384"}],"chars":13824,"notes":{}}
